\title{Structured Inputs & Multi-Output Shrinkage}

\subtree{
  \title{Parameter Coordinate Maps}

  \p{
    Domains passed to [baux.init](docs.html#baux.init) can be nested Python dicts, tuples, or dataclasses (\code{PyTree}s) combining positive scale variables, periodic angles, mixture weights, and discrete choices #{\mathcal{M} = \prod_{\ell=1}^L \mathcal{M}_\ell}. Each leaf specification maps parameter values #{x \in \mathcal{M}_\ell} into flat solver coordinates #{\psi_\ell : \mathcal{M}_\ell \to \mathcal{U}_\ell \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{d_\ell}} where neighbor distances and trust-region boxes are computed, and decodes proposals back via #{\rho_\ell : \mathcal{U}_\ell \to \mathcal{M}_\ell} with #{\rho_\ell(\psi_\ell(x)) = x}:
  }

  \ul{
    \li{
      \strong{Log-scale intervals} ([baux.Log](docs.html#baux.Log)): On #{[a, b] \subset \mathbb{R}_+},
      ##{
        \psi(x) = \frac{\log x - \log a}{\log b - \log a} \in [0, 1], \qquad \rho(u) = a\left(\frac{b}{a}\right)^{\Pi_{[0,1]}(u)},
      }
      so uniform steps in #{u} correspond to constant multiplicative ratios in #{x}.
    }
    \li{
      \strong{Periodic intervals} ([baux.Periodic](docs.html#baux.Periodic)): On #{\lbrack a, b\rparen} of period #{P = b - a} with phase #{\phi(x) = \tfrac{2\pi(x - a)}{P}}, circle embedding and #{\operatorname{atan2}} decoding,
      ##{
        \psi(x) = \left(\frac{1}{2} + \frac{\cos\phi(x)}{2\pi},\; \frac{1}{2} + \frac{\sin\phi(x)}{2\pi}\right), \qquad d_{\mathcal{U}}\bigl(\psi(x), \psi(x')\bigr) = \frac{1}{\pi}\left|\sin\left(\frac{\phi(x) - \phi(x')}{2}\right)\right|,
      }
      avoid artificial jumps across the wrap boundary #{a \equiv b}.
    }
    \li{
      \strong{Probability simplices} ([baux.Simplex](docs.html#baux.Simplex)): On #{\Delta^{d-1}}, the square-root map onto the positive unit sphere and radial normalization,
      ##{
        \psi(p) = \bigl(\sqrt{p_1}, \dots, \sqrt{p_d}\bigr) \in S_+^{d-1}, \qquad \rho(u) = \frac{\max(u, 0)^{\odot 2}}{\Vert\max(u, 0)\Vert_2^2},
      }
      make squared Euclidean distance in #{\mathcal{U}} equal to squared Hellinger distance #{\sum_{j=1}^d \bigl(\sqrt{p_j} - \sqrt{q_j}\bigr)^2}.
    }
    \li{
      \strong{Discrete sets} ([baux.categorical](docs.html#baux.categorical)): Finite choices #{\{1, \dots, C\}} map to equal-norm orthogonal or Rademacher codewords #{\psi(c) \in \mathbb{R}^{d_c}}, reducing nearest-codeword decoding to #{\rho(u) = \arg\max_c \langle u, \psi(c)\rangle}.
    }
  }
}

\subtree{
  \title{Linear Subspace Shrinkage}

  \p{
    When each evaluation returns #{M} noisy outputs #{y = \theta + \eta \in \mathbb{R}^M} concentrated near a low-rank subspace, [baux.Stiefel](docs.html#baux.Stiefel) orthonormalizes a basis matrix #{L \in \mathbb{R}^{M \times r}} (#{1 \le r < M}) into #{V = L(L^\top L)^{-1/2}} with orthogonal complement #{V_\perp}. Writing #{z_\parallel = V^\top y} and #{z_\perp = V_\perp^\top y}, a linear shrinkage estimator leaves components in #{\operatorname{col}(V)} unchanged and damps the orthogonal residual by #{\alpha \in [0, 1]} (Stein [[Ste56]](https://projecteuclid.org/ebooks/berkeley-symposium-on-mathematical-statistics-and-probability/Proceedings-of-the-Third-Berkeley-Symposium-on-Mathematical-Statistics-and/chapter/Inadmissibility-of-the-Usual-Estimator-for-the-Mean-of-a/bsmsp/1200501656), James & Stein [[JS61]](https://projecteuclid.org/ebooks/berkeley-symposium-on-mathematical-statistics-and-probability/Proceedings-of-the-Fourth-Berkeley-Symposium-on-Mathematical-Statistics-and/chapter/Estimation-with-Quadratic-Loss/bsmsp/1200512173)):
  }

  ##{
    \hat{\theta}_\alpha = V V^\top y + \alpha (I_M - V V^\top) y = V z_\parallel + \alpha V_\perp z_\perp.
  }

  \p{
    With zero-mean noise variances #{v_\parallel = \mathbb{E}\Vert V^\top \eta\Vert_2^2} and #{v_\perp = \mathbb{E}\Vert V_\perp^\top \eta\Vert_2^2 > 0} and off-subspace signal energy #{b_\perp^2 = \Vert V_\perp^\top \theta\Vert_2^2}, the mean squared error #{\mathcal{R}(\alpha) = \mathbb{E}\Vert\hat{\theta}_\alpha - \theta\Vert_2^2} is minimized at
  }

  ##{
    \alpha^* = \frac{b_\perp^2}{b_\perp^2 + v_\perp}, \qquad \mathcal{R}(\alpha^*) = v_\parallel + \frac{v_\perp b_\perp^2}{b_\perp^2 + v_\perp} < v_\parallel + v_\perp = \mathcal{R}(1).
  }

  \p{
    Under isotropic Gaussian noise in orthogonal dimension #{M - r \ge 3}, positive-part James–Stein shrinkage dominates #{\alpha = 1} uniformly ([[JS61]](https://projecteuclid.org/ebooks/berkeley-symposium-on-mathematical-statistics-and-probability/Proceedings-of-the-Fourth-Berkeley-Symposium-on-Mathematical-Statistics-and/chapter/Estimation-with-Quadratic-Loss/bsmsp/1200512173), Efron & Morris [[EM73]](https://doi.org/10.1080/01621459.1973.10481350)); [baux.stein](docs.html#baux.stein) applies the empirical moment plug-in #{\hat{\alpha} = \Vert z_\perp\Vert_2^2 / (\Vert z_\perp\Vert_2^2 + \hat{v}_\perp)} for any #{M - r \ge 1}. When held-out validation pairs are available, [baux.calibrate](docs.html#baux.calibrate) fits an affine variance rescaling and residual quantile multiplier to posterior standard errors.
  }

  \p{
    Below, a \code{PyTree} domain combining logarithmic gain, periodic phase, simplex filter weights, and a categorical window drives a noisy 6-channel response concentrated near a rank-2 frame:
  }

  \pre{
import math
import numpy as np
import baux

rng = np.random.default_rng(3)
space = {
    "gain": baux.Log(1e-3, 1.0),
    "phase": baux.Periodic(0.0, 2.0 * math.pi),
    "weights": baux.Simplex(4),
    "window": baux.categorical(["hann", "hamming", "blackman", "kaiser"]),
}
loadings = np.array([
    [1.0, 0.0],
    [0.8, 0.2],
    [0.2, 0.9],
    [-0.4, 0.7],
    [0.5, -0.5],
    [-0.3, -0.6],
])
frame = baux.Stiefel(loadings)
target_w = np.array([0.45, 0.30, 0.15, 0.10])
win_loss = {"hann": 0.0, "hamming": 0.04, "blackman": 0.08, "kaiser": 0.01}


def spectral_channels(p):
    g_err = math.log10(p["gain"] / 0.05)
    ph_err = math.sin(0.5 * (p["phase"] - 1.2))
    w_err = float(np.sum((np.sqrt(p["weights"]) - np.sqrt(target_w)) ** 2))
    latent = np.array([-(g_err**2 + w_err + win_loss[p["window"]]), -(ph_err**2)])
    return loadings @ latent + 0.15 * rng.normal(size=6)


pipe = (
    baux.enn(k=8, geometry="conformal")
    | baux.stein(frame)
    | baux.ucb(beta=1.5)
    | baux.regions(count=3)
)
state = baux.init(space, pipe, key=baux.key(19))
for _ in range(10):
    state, batch = baux.ask(state, num_arms=3)
    y = np.vstack([spectral_channels(p) for p in batch.params])
    state, mu_shrunk = baux.tell(state, batch, y)

assert mu_shrunk.shape == (3, 6) and state.y_obs.shape == (30, 6)
  }
}
